{{also|Mason-Dixon line}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|Mason–Dixon line}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|Mason-Dixon line|Mason and Dixon line|Mason's and Dixon's line}}

===Etymology===
Named after English astronomers {{w|Charles Mason}} (1728–1786) and {{w|Jeremiah Dixon}} (1733–1779).

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|head=[[Mason]]–[[Dixon]] [[line|Line]]}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} The boundary line between [[Pennsylvania]] and [[Maryland]], as run (1764–1767) by English astronomers {{w|Charles Mason}} and {{w|Jeremiah Dixon}}, that, before [[abolition]], defined part of the northern boundary of states in which slavery was permitted.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1776|author=[[w:Thomas Jefferson|Thomas Jefferson]]|title=The Virginia Constitution (Letter to Edmund Pendleton)|url=http://infomotions.com/etexts/literature/american/1700-1799/jefferson-letters-256.htm|location=Philadelphia|passage=I am indebted to you for a topic to deny to the Pensylvania claim to a line 39 complete degrees from the equator. As an advocate I shall certainly insist on it; but I wish they would compromise by an extension of '''Mason & Dixon's line'''. — They do not agree to the temporary line proposed by our assembly.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en|year=1779|year_published=1847|author=[[w:George Bryan|George Bryan]]|title=Life and Correspondence of [[w:Joseph Reed (jurist)|Joseph Reed]]{{...|: Military Secretary of Washington, at Cambridge, Adjutant-general of the Continental Army, Member of the Congress of the United States, and President of the Executive Council of the State of Pennsylvania.}}|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=ojujVebQcqYC&pg=PA134|chapter=1779|section=George Bryan to President Reed, Baltimore, August 31st, 1779|publisher=Lindsay and Blakiston|location=Philadelphia|editor=[[w:William Bradford Reed|William Bradford Reed]]|volume=2|page=134|passage=The Virginia gentlemen offer to divide exactly the 40th degree with us, {{...}} Perhaps we would be as well off with '''''Mason and Dixon's'' line''' continued.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1861|year_published=1862|author=[[w:Artemus Ward|Artemus Ward]]|title=His Book: with Many Comic Illustrations|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=HM3U_PgFgdsC&pg=PA204|chapter=Thrilling Scenes in Dixie|publisher=Corleton|location=New-York|page=204|passage=Suffysit to say I got across '''Mason & Dixie's line''' safe at last.}}
# The boundary between the [[free state|free]] and [[slave state]]s at the time of the [[American Civil War]], or between states with different segregationist policies in the [[Jim Crow]] era.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1833|year_published=1834|author=Charles Augustus Davis
|title=Letters of Jack Downing, Major{{...|, Downingville Militia, Second Brigade, to His Old Friend, [[w:Theodore Dwight (elder)|Mr. Dwight]], of The New-York Daily Advertiser.}}|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=28A_AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA36|chapter=Letter III{{...|: Cause of the sudden termination of the President's Eastern Tour—Dance at Downingville—Trying on the Gineral's coat (July 14, 1833)}}|publisher=Harper & brothers|location=New-York|page=36|passage={{...}} and he tell'd me Georgia would go for me, arter the Gineral, as soon as any north of '''mason and dickson'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1843|author=[[w:Charles Fenno Hoffman|Charles Fenno Hoffman]], John Holmes Agnew|title=[[w:The Knickerbocker|The Knickerbocker; Or, New-York Monthly Magazine]]|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=-_ozAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA185|chapter=Editor's Table|publisher=John Allen|location=New-York|volume=22|page=185|passage=The epistles are not dated far apart; and in the second, the writer, who dwelleth near ''''''Mason and Dixon,'''''' descants upon the awful climate hereabout in the summer months.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|section=Part I. The New Deal Order, 1930–1980|chapter=2|author=Gary Gerstle|authorlink=Gary Gerstle|publisher=Oxford University Press|title=The Rise and Fall of the Neoliberal Order{{...|: America and the World in the Free Market Era}}|location=New York|isbn=978-0-19-751964-6|passage=The Soviet press was disseminating in Africa and Asia stories about black children in the South being denied adequate schooling, black accident victims dying because no white hospital in the South would admit them, and African diplomats being refused access to white restaurants and washrooms while traveling south of the '''Mason-Dixon line'''.}}

